"""Chapter 5 drill items — development, population and unemployment."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_5 = [
    q("Economic growth is best defined as", ["an increase in real national output", "an improvement in welfare", "a rise in the general price level", "an increase in the population"], 0, "A rise in real output. Improving welfare is development, a rise in prices is inflation, and population growth is a change in the size of the economy's base, not in its output."),
    q("Economic development differs from economic growth because development", ["is measured in money while growth is not", "includes health, education and distribution as well as output", "is always faster than growth", "applies only to developed countries"], 1, "It is a wider concept. Growth is one number; development is a judged condition that includes health, schooling, employment and inequality."),
    q("A country whose real GDP rises while life expectancy falls and inequality widens is experiencing", ["development without growth", "growth without development", "both growth and development", "neither"], 1, "Growth without development: output rose, but welfare did not improve on two standard measures."),
    q("The three components of the human development index are", ["income per capita, life expectancy and schooling", "income per capita, population and employment", "inflation, unemployment and growth", "literacy, fertility and urbanisation"], 0, "Income, health and knowledge. The other options mix in variables the index does not use."),
    q("A country with a high income per capita but a low HDI most likely has", ["a large population", "poor health and education outcomes", "a trade surplus", "a fixed exchange rate"], 1, "The income has not translated into health or schooling — the classic case of growth without development, often with severe inequality."),
    q("Per capita income is obtained by dividing national income by the", ["labour force", "population", "number of households", "number of employed persons"], 1, "The population. A denominator of the labour force would give income per worker, a productivity figure rather than a welfare one."),
    q("The dependency ratio is calculated as", ["dependants ÷ working-age population × 100", "working-age population ÷ dependants × 100", "labour force ÷ population × 100", "unemployed ÷ labour force × 100"], 0, "Dependants over the working-age population. The second option inverts it, the third is the participation rate, and the fourth is the unemployment rate."),
    q("A country has 30 million people under 15, 60 million aged 15 to 64 and 10 million over 64. Its dependency ratio is", ["50 per cent", "67 per cent", "150 per cent", "25 per cent"], 1, "Dependants are 30 + 10 = 40 million; 40 ÷ 60 × 100 = 66.7 per cent. 50 per cent counts only the children, and 150 per cent inverts the ratio."),
    q("A high dependency ratio implies that", ["a large share of the population must be supported by each worker", "unemployment must be high", "the population is falling", "the labour force is shrinking"], 0, "Each worker supports more non-workers, so saving and investment are harder and public spending on schools and health is higher."),
    q("A population pyramid with a very broad base indicates", ["an ageing population", "a young and rapidly growing population", "a stable population", "a declining population"], 1, "A broad base means many children, which implies high dependency and a labour force that will grow quickly as those children reach working age."),
    q("The labour force consists of", ["all persons of working age", "the employed plus those actively seeking work", "the employed only", "all persons over fifteen"], 1, "Employed plus actively seeking. Full-time students, retirees and discouraged workers are outside it, and counting them would distort the unemployment rate."),
    q("The labour-force participation rate is", ["labour force ÷ working-age population × 100", "employed ÷ labour force × 100", "unemployed ÷ labour force × 100", "labour force ÷ total population × 100"], 0, "Labour force over working-age population. The second is the employment rate and the third is the unemployment rate."),
    q("An economy has 36 million employed, 4 million unemployed and actively seeking work. The unemployment rate is", ["4 per cent", "10 per cent", "11.1 per cent", "4.4 per cent"], 1, "4 ÷ (36 + 4) × 100 = 10 per cent. 4 per cent divides by the employed only, 11.1 per cent divides by the employed and then adds, and 4.4 per cent uses the wrong base again."),
    q("A person who is between jobs and looking for work is", ["frictionally unemployed", "structurally unemployed", "cyclically unemployed", "seasonally unemployed"], 0, "Frictional — the short search period in any working labour market. It is not caused by a skill mismatch or by the state of demand."),
    q("Unemployment caused by a permanent change in the structure of the economy is", ["frictional", "structural", "cyclical", "seasonal"], 1, "Structural: the skill or industry no longer exists where it is needed, so the cure is retraining and redeployment rather than stimulus."),
    q("Unemployment caused by a general fall in demand during a recession is", ["frictional", "structural", "cyclical", "seasonal"], 2, "Cyclical, also called demand-deficient. The cure is reflation, which is why the cause matters — the same symptom has different remedies."),
    q("A farmer who has no work between harvests is", ["frictionally unemployed", "structurally unemployed", "cyclically unemployed", "seasonally unemployed"], 3, "Seasonal, because the work follows a calendar. The cure is diversification and planning, not demand stimulus."),
    q("The appropriate cure for structural unemployment is", ["increased aggregate demand", "retraining and redeployment", "waiting for the business cycle to turn", "seasonal work planning"], 1, "Retraining, because the problem is a mismatch of skills. More demand would not create jobs for skills that no longer exist."),
    q("The appropriate cure for cyclical unemployment is", ["retraining programmes", "expansionary fiscal or monetary policy", "seasonal employment schemes", "reducing the retirement age"], 1, "Reflation. Demand is deficient, so the cure is to raise demand — the other options address different kinds of unemployment."),
    q("Underemployment is best described as", ["working fewer hours or below one's skill level than one would like", "being out of work and seeking it", "working for low wages", "working without a contract"], 0, "It counts as employment in the official figures even though the worker's capacity is not fully used, which is why the unemployment rate can understate the problem."),
    q("A country's unemployment rate can fall while its labour market deteriorates because", ["discouraged workers leave the labour force", "the population is growing", "wages are rising", "the exchange rate is fixed"], 0, "Discouraged workers stop looking, so they leave the labour force and stop being counted as unemployed — the participation rate is the figure that reveals it."),
    q("The demographic transition describes a change from", ["high birth and death rates to low birth and death rates", "low birth and death rates to high ones", "rural to urban living", "agriculture to manufacturing"], 0, "High-to-low on both rates, with the death rate falling first — which is why population grows fastest in the middle of the transition."),
    q("Population growth faster than output growth will", ["raise per capita income", "lower per capita income", "leave per capita income unchanged", "raise wages"], 1, "Per capita income falls, because the same or slower output is shared among more people. This is the arithmetic behind the population trap."),
    q("A country's population is growing at 3 per cent a year and its real GDP at 2 per cent. Per capita income is", ["rising by 5 per cent", "falling by about 1 per cent", "unchanged", "falling by 5 per cent"], 1, "Output grows more slowly than the number of people, so income per head falls by roughly the difference — about 1 per cent a year."),
    q("Which measure best captures inequality of income?", ["the Gini coefficient", "the human development index", "the consumer price index", "the dependency ratio"], 0, "The Gini coefficient, which runs from 0 for perfect equality to 1 for complete inequality. The others measure welfare, prices and age structure respectively."),
    q("Absolute poverty is defined by reference to", ["a fixed minimum standard of living", "the average income of the country", "inequality between rich and poor", "the level of unemployment"], 0, "A fixed standard — a poverty line. Relative poverty, by contrast, is defined against the living standards of the society concerned."),
    q("Investment in education is regarded as investment in", ["physical capital", "human capital", "natural capital", "financial capital"], 1, "Human capital: the skills and health embodied in people, which raise their productivity and the economy's output."),
    q("The main reason a developing economy has a large informal sector is that", ["the formal sector cannot absorb the labour force", "informal work pays better", "governments encourage it", "taxes are too low"], 0, "The formal sector absorbs only part of the labour force, so the rest creates its own work outside registration and taxation."),
    q("Rural-urban migration in developing economies is driven mainly by", ["higher expected wages and better services in the cities", "lower rents in the cities", "government relocation schemes", "cheaper food in the cities"], 0, "The expected wage gap and the services, even though the actual urban outcome for many migrants is informal work and poor housing."),
    q("An economy with a large youth population must above all provide", ["schools now and jobs when the cohort arrives", "pensions for the elderly", "imports of consumer goods", "a fixed exchange rate"], 0, "The two obligations run in sequence, and failing the second turns a demographic dividend into a demographic burden."),
    q("Which is a non-economic indicator of development?", ["literacy rate", "gross domestic product", "per capita income", "balance of payments"], 0, "Literacy is social. GDP, per capita income and the balance of payments are all monetary or output measures."),
    q("Development planning in a mixed economy typically aims to", ["replace the market entirely", "coordinate public investment and guide private activity", "abolish taxation", "fix all prices permanently"], 1, "Coordination and guidance. A plan in a mixed economy sets direction and public investment; it does not replace markets or fix every price."),
    q("The 'population trap' refers to a situation in which", ["population growth absorbs all the gains from growth", "population falls too fast", "birth rates are below replacement", "the labour force is too small"], 0, "Growth in output is swallowed by growth in numbers, so income per head never rises. It is the arithmetic of the previous question generalised over time."),
    q("Which of the following is a characteristic of a developing economy?", ["a high proportion of the labour force in agriculture", "a small informal sector", "low population growth", "complete mechanisation of farming"], 0, "Agriculture dominates employment, productivity is low, the informal sector is large and population growth is usually rapid."),
    q("Full employment in the economic sense means", ["every adult has a job", "there is no cyclical unemployment, though frictional and structural unemployment remain", "the unemployment rate is zero", "everyone works for the government"], 1, "No demand-deficient unemployment. Some frictional and structural unemployment is unavoidable, so a zero rate is not the target."),
    q("Which of these is NOT a cause of unemployment in a developing economy?", ["rapid population growth", "a mismatch between skills and jobs", "falling aggregate demand", "a high level of capital per worker"], 3, "High capital per worker raises productivity rather than causing unemployment; the other three are the standard causes."),
    q("Investing in labour-intensive industries in a country with surplus labour is intended to", ["raise output per worker quickly", "create employment alongside output", "reduce the wage rate", "increase imports"], 1, "It matches the technology to the endowment: with abundant labour, labour-intensive methods create more jobs per naira invested."),
    q("The Human Development Index is expressed as a number between", ["0 and 1", "0 and 100", "1 and 10", "−1 and 1"], 0, "Between 0 and 1, as a geometric mean of the three scaled components. The index is often reported to three decimal places."),
    q("A rise in a country's HDI with no change in GDP per capita could be caused by", ["improvements in health and schooling", "a rise in the price level", "a fall in unemployment", "a trade surplus"], 0, "Health and schooling improvements raise two of the three components directly, and the index can rise without any change in income."),
    q("Which statement about unemployment and inflation is correct?", ["a short-run trade-off exists but can shift", "the two always move in opposite directions", "the two are unrelated", "unemployment causes deflation always"], 0, "The Phillips-curve trade-off holds in the short run but shifts when expectations change, which is why it is a menu rather than a law."),
]
